'''''Ajuga pyramidalis''''', commonly known as '''pyramidal bugle''',<ref name=BSBI07>{{BSBI 2007 |access-date=2014-10-17 }}</ref> is a [[flowering plant]] of the genus ''[[Ajuga]]'' in the family [[Lamiaceae]]. It is a [[native plant]] in [[Europe]].

==Description==
Pyramidal bugle is a perennial, herbaceous plant growing from about {{convert|5|to|20|cm|0|abbr=on}} tall.  At the base there is a rosette of stalked leaves which are significantly larger than the stem leaves. The stiff upright hairy stem is square and bears simple leaves growing in opposite pairs. They are ovate, hairy above and below and have a slightly wavy edge. The [[inflorescence]] has leaf-like bracts subtending the individual flowers. The bracts gradually get smaller towards the tip of the inflorescence, are always longer than the flowers and the upper ones are often tinged purple. The inflorescence forms a pyramid-shaped terminal spike and is formed of axillary whorls. The calyx of each flower is five-lobed, the bluish-violet corolla has a long tube and is fused, with two lips. The upper lip is very short and the lower lip is three-lobed. There are four stamens, two long and two short. The [[gynoecium]] is formed of two fused carpels and the fruit is a four-chambered [[schizocarp]]. The hermaphrodite flowers are zygomorphic. The flowers produce nectar to attract pollinators which are bumblebees and butterflies.<ref name=NatureGate>{{cite web |url=http://www.luontoportti.com/suomi/en/kukkakasvit/pyramidal-bugle |title=Pyramidal Bugle: ''Ajuga pyramidalis'' |publisher=NatureGate |access-date=2013-12-29}}</ref>

The flowering time extends from June to August. The [[chromosome number]] is 2n = 32.

==Ecology==

The [[wikt:bract#Noun|bracts]] in the [[wikt:midrib#inflorescence|inflorescence]] shelter the flowers from rain, and their violet colour enhances the signal effect for pollinators. The [[wikt:calyx#Noun|calyx]] are haired, protecting the flower from crawling insects. Additionally, the nectar is secured by a stiff, upturned hair ring. The fruits contain fleshy, oily [[wikt:elaisome#Noun|elaiosomes]].

==Occurrence==
The pyramid bugle is native to in northern Europe and the Caucasus. It occurs in the mountains of central and southern Europe, and in northern and western Scandinavia.[http://linnaeus.nrm.se/flora/di/lamia/ajuga/ajugpyrv.jpg] 
In the British Isles, it ranges down to sea level, being very rare in Ireland<ref>Parnell, J. and Curtis, T. 2012. ''Webb's An Irish Flora''. Cork University Press. {{ISBN|978-185918-4783}}</ref> and otherwise occurring in Scotland and Westmoreland in England.<ref>Clapham, A.R., Tutin, T.G. and Warburg, E.F.v 1968. ''Excursion Flora of the British Isles''. Second Edition. Cambridge University Press. {{ISBN|0-521-04656-4}}</ref> It grows on almost neutral, preferentially rocky soils in open grassland, heathland and rock ledges.<ref name=Rich>{{cite journal|first1=T.C.G.|last1=Rich|first2=G.M.|last2=Kay|first3=C.|last3=Sydes|journal=Botanical Journal of Scotland|volume=51|year=1999|title=Distribution and ecology of pyramidal bugle (Ajuga pyramidalis L., Lamiaceae) in the British Isles|issue=2|doi=10.1080/03746609908684934|pages=181–193 }}</ref> In the [[Alps]], it grows at altitudes of up to {{convert|2700|m|0|abbr=on}}.

==Uses==
The pyramid bugle, like many Ajuga species,<ref>{{cite web|last=Essays |first=UK |url=https://www.ukessays.com/essays/biology/anatomical-studies-medicinal-taxon-ajuga-orientalis-1439.php?vref=1 |title=Ajuga Orientalis L Anatomy Studies |publisher=UKEssays.com |date=November 2013 |access-date=15 September 2018 |location=Nottingham, UK}}</ref><ref>{{cite journal |title= Plants and People of Nepal |author= Manandhar. N.P |year= 2002 }}</ref><ref>{{cite web |url=http://www.naturalmedicinalherbs.net/herbs/a/ajuga-decumbens.php |title= Medicinal use of Ajuga decumbens |website=naturalmedicinalherbs.net |access-date= 15 September 2018}}</ref> is claimed to hold [[medicinal]] properties and has been used for wound treatment and for metabolic disorders.<ref>{{cite web |url=http://www.botanical.com/botanical/mgmh/b/buglec82.html |title= Bugle, Common |website=botanical.com |access-date= 15 September 2018}}</ref><ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.newlifeonahomestead.com/medicinal-weed-ajuga-bugleweed/ |title= Medicinal Weed: Ajuga, aka Bugleweed |website=newlifeonahomestead.com |access-date= 15 September 2018}}</ref><ref>Howard, Michael (1987). ''Traditional Folk Remedies'' Century. p.95</ref>

